{"chunk_id": "5f5b0c085ee8", "text": "This passage presents a philosophical teaching from Vyasa to his son Suka on the highest duty and the path to liberation. True wisdom, it says, frees a person from fear and attachment to worldly pleasures, which are seen as inherently flawed. The supreme duty is the restraint of the senses and mind through understanding, withdrawing them from unworthy objects and directing them inward. Through disciplined concentration, a person perceives the eternal Self within, distinct from the body and transient experiences. Vyasa illustrates life as a dangerous river filled with the senses, desires, and delusions that threaten to drown the soul. Only knowledge, purified understanding, and detachment allow one to cross it. The ultimate realization is knowledge of Brahman\u2014beyond gender, pleasure, or pain\u2014which grants emancipation and freedom from rebirth.\nVyasa teaches Suka that the highest duty is restraining the senses and realizing the all\u2011pervading Soul, crossing the perilous river of worldly existence to attain emancipation.\nThemes: self\u2011control and restraint, knowledge of the Self, liberation from rebirth, teacher\u2011disciple transmission, river of life metaphor", "vector": [0.013956503942608833, -0.005733337253332138, -0.007765576709061861, -0.011648365296423435, -0.024750251322984695, 0.019595632329583168, -0.020766524598002434, -0.002636191202327609, -0.02742850035429001, -0.02037622779607773, -0.004801333416253328, 0.01927262730896473, 0.007186859380453825, 0.019245710223913193, 0.003566512605175376, -0.00013290306378621608, 0.05114244669675827, -0.00883553083986044, 0.014858226291835308, -0.015194689854979515, -0.0066989874467253685, 0.008875906467437744, 0.009952588938176632, -0.012247269973158836, -0.018303612247109413, 0.008310647681355476, 0.023754321038722992, -0.034184686839580536, 0.0047777811996638775, -0.03730706870555878, 0.008128956891596317, -0.0013517419574782252, -0.007637720555067062, -0.00406